{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat -n 'docs/design/datom.world.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n    19\t   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n    20\t   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n    21\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    22\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    25\t\n    26\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    27\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    28\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    29\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    30\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    31\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    34\t\n    35\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    36\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    37\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    38\t\n    39\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Streams\n    42\t\n    43\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    44\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    45\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    46\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    47\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    48\t\n    49\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    50\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    51\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    52\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    53\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    54\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    55\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    56\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    57\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    58\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    59\tthe stream's own.\n    60\t\n    61\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    62\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    63\t\n    64\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    65\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    66\t\n    67\t### Host Boundaries\n    68\t\n    69\tA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n    70\tsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n    71\texhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n    72\t\n    73\t**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n    74\thappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n    75\tappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n    76\tidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n    77\tno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n    78\tcorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n    79\t\n    80\t**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n    81\ta timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    82\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    83\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    84\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    85\t\n    86\t**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n    87\tadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n    88\ttimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n    89\twhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n    90\tthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n    91\tto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n    92\tdeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n    93\tmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n    94\tnot an upward callback.\n    95\t\n    96\tHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n    97\tplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n    98\ttransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n    99\tadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n   100\tevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n   101\tfor facts it owns.\n   102\t\n   103\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   104\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   105\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n   106\tthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n   107\tconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   108\t\n   109\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n   110\tnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n   111\thappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n   112\tevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n   113\t(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n   114\tdoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n   115\tnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n   116\tinterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n   117\t\n   118\tTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n   119\tcall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n   120\tappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n   121\tcallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n   122\tthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n   123\tdirections wearing the shape of an event.\n   124\t\n   125\t## Architectural Commitments\n   126\t\n   127\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n   128\t- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n   129\t- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n   130\t- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n   131\t\n   132\t## Design Principles\n   133\t\n   134\tCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n   135\tStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n   136\t\n   137\t**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\n   138\tcomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\n   139\twithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n   140\t\n   141\t- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n   142\t  ordinary streams.\n   143\t- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n   144\t  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n   145\t- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n   146\t- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n   147\t  auto-index observer.\n   148\t- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n   149\t  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n   150\t\n   151\t**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n   152\tcanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n   153\tshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n   154\t`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n   155\tquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n   156\tconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n   157\tretired `:global` tag in\n   158\t[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n   159\tcase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n   160\t\n   161\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   162\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   163\t\n   164\t## Code Style\n   165\t\n   166\t- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n   167\t- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n   168\t- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n   169\t- Avoid cleverness.\n   170\t- Name things after what they do to streams.\n   171\t\n   172\t## Development Workflow\n   173\t\n   174\t- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n   175\t- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n   176\t- Do not optimize prematurely.\n   177\t- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n   178\t- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n   179\t- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n   180\t  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n   181\t  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n   182\t  leaves to review.\n   183\t\n   184\t### Problem Decomposition\n   185\t\n   186\tIf a problem appears complex:\n   187\t1. Decompose it into streams.\n   188\t2. Identify invariants.\n   189\t3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n   190\t4. Make causality explicit.\n   191\t5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n   192\t\n   193\t## Operational & Specialized Guides\n   194\t\n   195\t- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n   196\t- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n   197\t- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n   198\t- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n   199\t- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat -n 'docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat -n 'docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm.linker over dao.jing.dht: publish, load and evaluate code by name\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **contract frozen 2026-10-01 (linker-over-DHT epic slice L-design);\n     4\tnothing is implemented.** Section 2 records the starting tree. Section 12\n     5\tis the slice plan, L0 to L5. Subordinate to\n     6\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md),\n     7\t[`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md),\n     8\t[`dao.jing.dht.md`](./dao.jing.dht.md),\n     9\t[`yin.repl.link-policy.md`](./yin.repl.link-policy.md) and\n    10\t[`yin.repl.dao.space-index.md`](./yin.repl.dao.space-index.md). In sections\n    11\t3 to 11 every sentence is a rule.\n    12\t\n    13\tInputs: the lead design\n    14\t(`collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`),\n    15\tthe second opinion\n    16\t(`collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht-second-opinion.gpt-6-sol.findings.md`,\n    17\tAGREE-WITH-CHANGES, corrections adopted), and the owner decisions of\n    18\t2026-10-01\n    19\t(`collab/1790808000000-orchestrator-linker-over-dht-owner-decisions.md`).\n    20\t\n    21\t## 1. Overview and scope\n    22\t\n    23\tDHT epic S5 gave any Clojure program, and `yin.repl` through host functions,\n    24\tone path to join the DHT, load a published covered index from its manifest\n    25\taddress, and query it. Loading code *for evaluation* is a different path: the\n    26\tlinker resolves a module name to a module manifest address through a name\n    27\tenvironment, and links the manifest's image over a content pair. This\n    28\tdocument joins the two.\n    29\t\n    30\tIt answers one question: how does a second process, attached to the same\n    31\tDHT, load a module by name and evaluate it, with every trusted claim stated?\n    32\t\n    33\tOwner invariants this contract is bound by:\n    34\t\n    35\t- \"plain clojure code should be able to query for code in the dht. the\n    36\t  yin.repl should use the same path as the clojure repl via host-functions\"\n    37\t  (2026-10-01). Every operation here is a plain Clojure function;\n    38\t  `yin.repl` adapts effects to those functions and holds no second loader.\n    39\t- Code is shared over the stream linker; the linker boundary is\n    40\t  `dao.stream`; local versus remote linking is the composition's choice of\n    41\t  streams.\n    42\t- `dao.stream` is peer to peer: no server, no client, no privileged node.\n    43\t  Publishing and trusting are per-node composition, never network roles.\n    44\t- Derive, don't persist: nothing is stored that a query over existing rows\n    45\t  derives.\n    46\t- `dao.jing` is syntax and agents are semantics: the store stays passive and\n    47\t  payload-agnostic.\n    48\t- Code and continuations are datoms.\n    49\t\n    50\t**Out of scope:** latest-root discovery, key rotation and revocation, the\n    51\tpersistent stepped linker, the `:lease` link policy, authenticated node ids.\n    52\tSection 13 lists every deferral.\n    53\t\n    54\t## 2. The starting tree (master `c66809fa`)\n    55\t\n    56\t| Fact | Where |\n    57\t|---|---|\n    58\t| A link attempt builds a fresh link state and fresh cursors, runs `link-manifest` synchronously for at most 64 drive rounds, and reports `:pending`. The `:remote` drive advances only a counter. | `src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:167-227` |\n    59\t| A `dao.space.dht` node's byte-store handle reads `:local` only. `load-index` stages a miss around the handle: walk against a probing handle, fetch the missing blob with `:jing/get`, walk again. | `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:457-597`; `dao.jing.dht.md` 5.1 |\n    60\t| The REPL's index holds AST datoms with `:yin/address` facts naming row addresses. The rows themselves are never stored: `yin.vm/materialize-tree!` has no caller in `src/`. | `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc`; `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1472` |\n    61\t| A REPL definition is the application `(yin/def 'f value)`: operator the variable `yin/def`, first operand a literal symbol. Definitions are found by query; no definition-name fact exists. | `yin/vm.cljc:296`; `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` |\n    62\t| `yin.vm.linker/publish!` stores one format image. Module manifests are assembled only by test helpers. | `linker.cljc:1992`; `test/yin/repl/require_test.cljc:134` |\n    63\t| The authority fold (`yin.vm.linker.md` 8.2) is implemented and wired into nothing. The REPL's name environment is a plain map. | `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc`; `yin/repl.cljc:862` |\n    64\t| The fold resolves a name when every accepted assertion names the same address, keeping all asserters; it refuses only on more than one distinct address. | `authority.cljc:226-255` |\n    65\t| `yin.repl/recheck-pending` exists; no host driver calls it. A parked require progresses only on a typed line. | `yin/repl.cljc:1848`; `yin/repl/main.cljc:286` |\n    66\t| The `dao.space.dht` host module answers `load-index`, `load-status` and `q` as effects on the query call pair, threading the node through `query/serve`. | `src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:127-153, 570-616` |\n    67\t| A module whose export reads another module-level definition from inside a lambda body links on the stack and register backends only; the tree scanners retain the read and the manifest cannot declare it. | `require_test.cljc:98-106`; `yin.vm.linker.md` 4.2 step 5a |\n    68\t| `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` is deterministic CBOR, identical across hosts. | `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:279` |\n    69\t| A DHT node holds at most 64 pending writes; a write beyond that is `/busy`. | `dao.jing.dht.md` 9 |\n    70\t\n    71\t## 3. Rulings\n    72\t\n    73\t| # | Ruling | Source |\n    74\t|---|---|---|\n    75\t| R1 | The linker's content source is the `dao.space.dht` node's own store handle, the `:kind :local` row of `yin.vm.linker.md` 6.1. A module's closure is loaded onto the node first, by an explicit closure walker (section 4). The node stays the only step owner; no linker drive steps it. | lead; second opinion |\n    76\t| R2 | Two things are published: the code index (facts) and the module closure (content). The name binding is one signed envelope in the index. No datom family is added for images. | lead; second opinion |\n    77\t| R3 | Every evaluated program's rows are materialized into the index store every round. An index `:yin/address` fact names retrievable content. | owner decision 1 |\n    78\t| R4 | A name is resolved from signed envelopes in explicitly loaded index snapshots, under the fold of `yin.vm.linker.md` 8.2. No name-environment root is published. Direct manifest addresses stay admissible without any signature. | lead; second opinion |\n    79\t| R5 | The proof kind for a DHT-discovered name is a signature. An attested-log proof does not survive this transport. | lead; second opinion |\n    80\t| R6 | Signatures are Ed25519 on the JVM, Node and Dart, with cross-host vectors. Canonical signed bytes, key encoding and the principal id are fixed in section 6 before any adapter is written. | owner decision 3; second opinion |\n    81\t| R7 | A publisher's key is stable and loaded from a file. Loss and replacement are defined in section 6.5. Tests may use ephemeral keys. | owner decision 2 |\n    82\t| R8 | The same name bound to the same address by several declared principals resolves, with every asserter in provenance. Different addresses refuse `:ambiguous-name`. | owner decision 4 |\n    83\t| R9 | A module is published explicitly, by name and export list, with its tree derived from indexed code. | adopted recommendation |\n    84\t| R10 | A `require` under a DHT link source may start a bounded load. The banner and the help say so. Pending and failure are data. | adopted recommendation |\n    85\t| R11 | The host ticker re-checks a pending run only when a load that run waits on completes or fails. | second opinion |\n    86\t| R12 | A DHT request budget is not a lease (section 8.3). | second opinion |\n    87\t\n    88\t## 4. The content source\n    89\t\n    90\t### 4.1 Composition\n    91\t\n    92\tA DHT link source is `{:kind :dht}` in `yin.repl.link/composition`, chosen by\n    93\t`yin.repl/create-state` when the index store is `dht:<dir>`. It holds no\n    94\thandle of its own: every serve round receives the shell's node and reads\n    95\tcontent through `(dao.space.dht/store node)`, which reads `:local` only.\n    96\t`:content-store` and `:content-client` remain, for the other rows of\n    97\t`yin.vm.linker.md` 6.1, and are refused together with a DHT store.\n    98\t\n    99\tOne link attempt against a DHT source proceeds in this order:\n   100\t\n   101\t1. Resolve the name to a module manifest address (section 7.3). No\n   102\t   resolution: refuse with the fold's outcome.\n   103\t2. Read the module load record for that address on the node (4.3).\n   104\t   - None: start the load, answer pending.\n   105\t   - `:loading`: answer pending.\n   106\t   - `:failed`: refuse (section 9), then `forget` the record, so a later\n   107\t     require starts a new load.\n   108\t   - `:loaded`: continue.\n   109\t3. Link with `link-manifest` over a local runtime on the node's store,\n   110\t   `:derivation :verifying`, `:defer-discharge true`.\n   111\t\n   112\tA link that follows a `:loaded` record issues no content request to any\n   113\tpeer. A link refusal `:absent` after `:loaded` is a walker defect and is\n   114\treported as one (`:yin.link.dht/closure-incomplete`), never retried silently.\n   115\t\n   116\t`yin.vm.linker/local-runtime` is the one function that turns a `dao.jing`\n   117\thandle into a link runtime `{:state :drive}` over an in-process ring pair\n   118\tserved by `dao.jing.content/serve-step`. `yin.repl.link` and both test\n   119\thelpers call it.\n   120\t\n   121\t**Why not a content client on the node's rings.** The node's request and\n   122\tanswer rings are the `:jing/*` convention, so the shape admits it. The\n   123\tattempt lifecycle does not: an answer the DHT produces on a later tick lands\n   124\tfor a link state that no longer exists. That composition needs\n   125\t`link-manifest` as a persistent stepped state machine and is deferred\n   126\t(section 13).\n   127\t\n   128\t### 4.2 The module closure walker\n   129\t\n   130\t`yin.vm.linker.closure/walk` is a pure function over a `dao.jing` handle. It\n   131\treads with `dao.jing/get` and a sentinel, performs no stream operation, and\n   132\tnever throws for absent or invalid content.\n   133\t\n   134\t```clojure\n   135\t(walk handle manifest-address)\n   136\t(walk handle manifest-address {:bounds {:max-parts n :max-depth d :max-bytes b}})\n   137\t\n   138\t;; exactly one of:\n   139\t{:yin.link.closure/outcome :missing\n   140\t :address a :role role :path [...]}\n   141\t{:yin.link.closure/outcome :complete\n   142\t :manifest manifest-address :blobs n :modules [manifest-address ...]}\n   143\t{:yin.link.closure/outcome :invalid\n   144\t :address a :role role :path [...] :defect {...}}\n   145\t```\n   146\t\n   147\t`role` is one of `:manifest`, `:row`, `:record`, `:image`, `:require`.\n   148\t`path` is the chain of manifest addresses from the root to the module the\n   149\tblob belongs to.\n   150\t\n   151\tThe closure of one manifest is, in walk order:\n   152\t\n   153\t| Step | Blob | Check before continuing |\n   154\t|---|---|---|\n   155\t| 1 | The manifest | `segment-matches?`; `manifest-defect` is nil. |\n   156\t| 2 | Every row of `:yin.module/tree` | A bounded worklist over the grammar's child slots (`ast-format`'s `:parts-fn`); each row `segment-matches?` and has no `row-local-defect`. |\n   157\t| 3 | The derivation record of each of `:yin.semantic/code`, `:yin.debruijn.code`, `:yin.debruijn.register` the manifest names | `segment-matches?`; `record-defect` is nil; `:yin.ledger/input` equals the tree. |\n   158\t| 4 | The image each record's `:yin.ledger/output` identifies | Its storage address is the identity itself for the semantic vector, and the `:yin.module/index` entry for H and R; an identity with no index entry is `:invalid`. The image satisfies its format record's `:identity-matches-fn`. |\n   159\t| 5 | The closure of every manifest under `:yin.module/requires` | Recursively, depth bounded; a manifest already visited is not walked again. |\n   160\t\n   161\t- **All four formats are walked, whichever one a request names.** A closure\n   162\t  is complete only when the tree and all three lowered images the manifest\n   163\t  names are local. A manifest that names fewer derivations has a smaller\n   164\t  closure; that is the publisher's choice and section 5.2 forbids it to this\n   165\t  epic's publisher.\n   166\t- `:yin.module/primitives` values are profile addresses compared at\n   167\t  discharge. They are not fetched and are not part of the closure.\n   168\t- The first absent blob ends the walk as `:missing`. The first failed check\n   169\t  ends it as `:invalid`. `:invalid` is terminal; fetching again cannot\n   170\t  repair content that hashes to its address and is wrong.\n   171\t- A bound exceeded is `:invalid` with defect `{:rule :parts-limit}`.\n   172\t- The walker verifies shape and address. It does not re-lower, discharge or\n   173\t  judge names: `link-manifest` does, afterwards, against the same local\n   174\t  store.\n   175\t\n   176\t### 4.3 The staged load\n   177\t\n   178\t`dao.space.dht/load` generalizes `load-index`. `dao.space.dht` learns nothing\n   179\tabout `yin.*`: the walk is an argument.\n   180\t\n   181\t```clojure\n   182\t(dao.space.dht/load node address {:kind k :walk f})   ; -> node\n   183\t(dao.space.dht/load-status node address)              ; -> status or nil\n   184\t(dao.space.dht/forget node address)                   ; -> node\n   185\t```\n   186\t\n   187\t`f` is `(fn [handle] outcome)`, called with the node's local store, and\n   188\tanswers one of three shapes:\n   189\t\n   190\t```clojure\n   191\t{:dao.space.dht/walk :missing  :address a}\n   192\t{:dao.space.dht/walk :complete :value v}\n   193\t{:dao.space.dht/walk :invalid  :defect d}\n   194\t```\n   195\t\n   196\t- A function that throws is `:invalid` with the exception's message as the\n   197\t  defect.\n   198\t- `load-index` is `load` with `:kind :dao.space.dht/index` and a walk that\n   199\t  adapts `index-datoms`; its behaviour, events and tests are unchanged.\n   200\t- On `:missing` the node requests the address with one `:jing/get` through\n   201\t  its `dao.jing.content.step` client and holds one outstanding fetch per\n   202\t  load. A fetched payload is verified against the requested address before\n   203\t  it enters `:local` (`dao.jing/accept-bytes!`, `dao.jing.dht.md` 4.5), and\n   204\t  the walk re-reads and re-checks it.\n   205\t- A load is `:loaded` only when its walk answered `:complete`.\n   206\t- A load already `:loading`, `:loaded` or `:failed` is left as it is.\n   207\t  `forget` removes a terminal record; it is refused for a `:loading` one.\n   208\t\n   209\t**Load status:**\n   210\t\n   211\t```clojure\n   212\t{:status :loading :kind k :fetched n :fetching address-or-nil}\n   213\t{:status :loaded  :kind k :fetched n :value v}\n   214\t{:status :failed  :kind k :fetched n :reason reason}\n   215\t```\n   216\t\n   217\t**Failure reasons are data.** S5's strings are replaced, with no\n   218\tcompatibility path:\n   219\t\n   220\t| `:reason` | Meaning |\n   221\t|---|---|\n   222\t| `{:dao.space.dht/failure :miss :address a :cause c}` | No peer produced `a`. `c` is the `/miss` fact's reason for that address (`/exhausted`, `/deadline`, `/solo`, `/busy`), or `:dao.jing.dht/gap` when the fact was lost. |\n   223\t| `{:dao.space.dht/failure :invalid :address a :defect d}` | The walk refused local content. |\n   224\t| `{:dao.space.dht/failure :unaskable :address a :outcome o}` | The request could not be appended. |\n   225\t\n   226\t**Events** (from `step`, each a plain map under `:dao.space.dht/event`):\n   227\t\n   228\t```clojure\n   229\t{:dao.space.dht/event :loaded      :manifest a :kind k :fetched n ...}\n   230\t{:dao.space.dht/event :load-failed :manifest a :kind k :reason reason}\n   231\t```\n   232\t\n   233\tAn index load's `:loaded` event keeps `:datoms`. Each load emits exactly one\n   234\tterminal event. Rendering reasons as text is the REPL's (`yin.repl.dht`).\n   235\t\n   236\tA module load is `load` with `:kind :yin.module/manifest` and the walker of\n   237\t4.2. Not found from a DHT is not authoritative absence: a failed load says\n   238\tno reachable candidate produced a verifiable value in time, and a new\n   239\trequire may try again.\n   240\t\n   241\t## 5. Publication\n   242\t\n   243\t### 5.1 Rows, every round (owner decision 1)\n   244\t\n   245\tThe code indexer (`yin.repl.index`) materializes every row of each expanded\n   246\tprogram packet it commits, through `yin.vm/materialize-tree!`, into the\n   247\tindex store, in the same round as that packet's transaction and before the\n   248\tround's publication is announced.\n   249\t\n   250\t- The rows of a round belong to that round's publication: they are put\n   251\t  through the node's store and are in the announced window.\n   252\t- A packet the indexer did not commit materializes nothing.\n   253\t- A row put that fails is recorded under the indexer's `:failure` with\n   254\t  stage `:materialize`; the round is not reported indexed.\n   255\t- Restored history is not re-materialized on rehydration. An index\n   256\t  published before this slice names rows its store does not hold, and\n   257\t  loading one such program by tree address is `:absent`.\n   258\t\n   259\t**The banner changes.** With `--dht-publish` the REPL states that the code\n   260\tindex *and the code itself* (every evaluated program's rows) are shared.\n   261\t\n   262\t**Pacing.** A round now puts one blob per distinct new row. `dao.space.dht`\n   263\ttherefore holds a replicate backlog: a write the DHT answers `/busy` is\n   264\tappended again on a later step, at most the DHT's pending-write bound\n   265\toutstanding, and is never reported while the backlog holds it. A\n   266\tpublication is reported once, when every blob is `/sent` or refused for a\n   267\treason other than `/busy`. A round with more puts than the ledger ring\n   268\tholds is reported `:publication-unknown`, as today.\n   269\t\n   270\t### 5.2 A module closure\n   271\t\n   272\t`yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!` is the one manifest publisher. It is\n   273\tplain Clojure and host neutral, and takes any `dao.jing` handle.\n   274\t\n   275\t```clojure\n   276\t(publish-module! handle\n   277\t  {:name 'my.lib\n   278\t   :ast ast                       ; the canonical map AST\n   279\t   :exports #{'f 'g}\n   280\t   :requires {'other.lib manifest-address}\n   281\t   :primitives {'+ profile-address}\n   282\t   :footprint {:store-keys #{...} :effects #{...}}})\n   283\t;; ->\n   284\t{:address manifest-address\n   285\t :manifest {...}\n   286\t :identities {:yin.semantic/code v :yin.debruijn.code H :yin.debruijn.register R}\n   287\t :links {:yin.ast/code outcome :yin.semantic/code outcome\n   288\t         :yin.debruijn.code outcome :yin.debruijn.register outcome}}\n   289\t```\n   290\t\n   291\t- It materializes the tree rows, the semantic vector, the stack image, the\n   292\t  register image, one derivation record per lowered format under the pinned\n   293\t  profile (`ledger/lowering-profile`, `linker/stack-lowering-profile`,\n   294\t  `linker/register-lowering-profile`), and the schema-1 manifest with\n   295\t  `:yin.module/index` naming H and R.\n   296\t- **It always mints all four formats.** A manifest from this publisher has a\n   297\t  full closure.\n   298\t- **It links what it published.** After minting, it walks the closure\n   299\t  (must be `:complete`) and links each format locally under `:verifying`\n   300\t  with discharge deferred. `:links` carries each outcome: `{:status :ok}` or\n   301\t  the linker's refusal. A publisher learns before any peer does which\n   302\t  backends can link the module.\n   303\t- It refuses, writing nothing, when `:exports` names a symbol the tree does\n   304\t  not define at module level (`:yin.link.publish/undefined-export`).\n   305\t- Publishing twice writes nothing new: every blob is content-addressed.\n   306\t\n   307\t### 5.3 Deriving a module from indexed code (R9)\n   308\t\n   309\t`yin.vm.linker.publish/module-from-index` builds `publish-module!`'s\n   310\targument from a queryable index and a name. It persists nothing.\n   311\t\n   312\t```clojure\n   313\t(module-from-index db {:name 'my.lib :exports '[f g]\n   314\t                       :primitives vm/primitives :modules linked-modules})\n   315\t;; -> the publish-module! spec, or\n   316\t{:yin.link.publish/refused reason ...}\n   317\t```\n   318\t\n   319\t- **Defining program.** The defining program of a symbol is the indexed\n   320\t  program, greatest `t`, whose tree holds a `yin/def` application with that\n   321\t  literal symbol at module level. A symbol with none is\n   322\t  `:yin.link.publish/undefined-export`.\n   323\t- **Closure by definitions.** Start from the exports' defining programs. A\n   324\t  free name of the collected programs that some indexed program defines\n   325\t  adds that name's defining program. Repeat to a fixed point.\n   326\t- **One tree.** The module tree is the collected programs in ascending `t`,\n   327\t  each once, sequenced in that order. Whole programs are taken; a\n   328\t  definition that is not exported is module-internal.\n   329\t- **Free names after closure.** A remaining free name must be a primitive\n   330\t  of `:primitives` (declared under `:yin.module/primitives` with its profile\n   331\t  address) or a name qualified by a module in `:modules` that the session\n   332\t  linked through the linker (declared under `:yin.module/requires` with\n   333\t  that module's manifest address). Anything else refuses\n   334\t  `:yin.link.publish/undeclared-free` naming it.\n   335\t- A name bound by a host module refuses `:yin.link.publish/host-module`.\n   336\t  No host function arrives by linking.\n   337\t- `:footprint` is computed as `yin.vm.dependency-completion.md` requires.\n   338\t  An engineer who finds that computation unavailable for a derived tree\n   339\t  stops and reports; an empty footprint is not a default.\n   340\t\n   341\t**What links where.** Step 5a of `yin.vm.linker.md` is unchanged by this\n   342\tepic. A module whose exported lambda reads another module-level definition\n   343\tis published with `:links` showing the tree and semantic formats refused and\n   344\tthe stack and register formats ok, exactly as the starting tree behaves.\n   345\tSuch a module loads and evaluates on the stack and register VMs. The\n   346\tpublisher prints which.\n   347\t\n   348\t### 5.4 Announcing\n   349\t\n   350\t`(yin.vm.linker.dht/publish! node spec)` is `publish-module!` on\n   351\t`(dao.space.dht/store node)`. The blobs join the node's current publication\n   352\twindow and are reported by the announcement that closes it. A module\n   353\tpublished at the REPL is announced by the round's HEAD write, with the\n   354\tname assertion of section 6 in the same index.\n   355\t\n   356\t## 6. Names: envelopes, proofs, keys\n   357\t\n   358\t### 6.1 The envelope\n   359\t\n   360\tThe envelope shapes are `yin.vm.linker.md` 8.2's, with the key set closed\n   361\tand the principal fixed:\n   362\t\n   363\t```clojure\n   364\t;; assertion\n   365\t{:yin.module/op          :assert\n   366\t :yin.module/name        'my.lib\n   367\t :yin.module/manifest    :segment/...\n   368\t :yin.module/asserted-by \"ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"\n   369\t :yin.module/seq         41}\n   370\t\n   371\t;; retraction\n   372\t{:yin.module/op          :retract\n   373\t :yin.module/of          :segment/...        ; segment-key of the assertion\n   374\t :yin.module/asserted-by \"ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"\n   375\t :yin.module/seq         42}\n   376\t```\n   377\t\n   378\t- No other key is written. `:yin.module/issued-at` is not written: the\n   379\t  publisher owns no clock.\n   380\t- An envelope's id is its `dao.jing/segment-key`.\n   381\t- Envelopes enter the index as one transaction of datoms\n   382\t  `[ev :yin.module/envelope env]` and `[ev :yin.module/proof proof]`, one\n   383\t  entity per envelope, with the session's metadata entity in `m`.\n   384\t\n   385\t### 6.2 The principal id and key encoding\n   386\t\n   387\t- A principal **is** its public key: the string `\"ed25519:\"` followed by the\n   388\t  64 lowercase hexadecimal characters of the 32-byte Ed25519 public key\n   389\t  (RFC 8032 encoding). No name, hash or registry stands between them.\n   390\t- A public key in a declaration or a flag is the same 64-character hex.\n   391\t- A signature is the 64-byte RFC 8032 signature as 128 lowercase hex\n   392\t  characters.\n   393\t- A secret is the 32-byte RFC 8032 seed as 64 lowercase hex characters.\n   394\t- Any other length, any uppercase character, or any non-hex character is\n   395\t  refused. Decoding is strict.\n   396\t- A human label for a principal is local composition data (a petname). It\n   397\t  never enters an envelope.\n   398\t\n   399\t### 6.3 The proof and the signed bytes\n   400\t\n   401\t```clojure\n   402\t{:yin.module/signature \"<128 lowercase hex>\"}\n   403\t```\n   404\t\n   405\tThe signed message is exactly:\n   406\t\n   407\t```text\n   408\tUTF-8(\"yin.module/envelope:v1\\n\") || dao.jing/canonical-bytes(envelope)\n   409\t```\n   410\t\n   411\t- The prefix is domain separation: the key signs nothing else under this\n   412\t  prefix, and a signature made for another purpose is never an envelope\n   413\t  proof.\n   414\t- `canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing`'s deterministic CBOR. The same envelope\n   415\t  yields the same bytes on every host.\n   416\t- Ed25519 is pure (no prehash), per RFC 8032. Signing is deterministic: one\n   417\t  key and one envelope have one signature.\n   418\t- Verification is synchronous on every host. The fold calls it as\n   419\t  `(verify key canonical-bytes signature)`; the prefix is applied inside the\n   420\t  verify function, so `yin.vm.linker.authority` is unchanged.\n   421\t- Verification refuses, as `:bad-proof`: a signature that does not verify, a\n   422\t  malformed key or signature, and an envelope whose\n   423\t  `:yin.module/asserted-by` is not `\"ed25519:\"` plus the declared key.\n   424\t- **A change of `dao.jing`'s canonical encoder invalidates every existing\n   425\t  signature.** Envelopes are then re-signed by their principals. No\n   426\t  migration path exists and none is promised.\n   427\t\n   428\tA reader's declaration for one principal:\n   429\t\n   430\t```clojure\n   431\t{\"ed25519:<hex>\" {:proof :yin.module/signature\n   432\t                  :key \"<64 hex>\"\n   433\t                  :verify yin.vm.linker.sign/verify-envelope\n   434\t                  :seq-floor 0}}\n   435\t```\n   436\t\n   437\t### 6.4 `yin.vm.linker.sign`\n   438\t\n   439\t| Function | Meaning |\n   440\t|---|---|\n   441\t| `(generate)` | A new key `{:seed hex :public hex}` from the host CSPRNG. |\n   442\t| `(public-of seed)` | The public key of a seed. |\n   443\t| `(principal public)` | `\"ed25519:\" + public`. |\n   444\t| `(sign-envelope seed envelope)` | The proof map of 6.3. |\n   445\t| `(verify-envelope public canonical-bytes signature)` | True or false; never throws. |\n   446\t| `(key-text key)`, `(key-from-text s)` | The key file's content, both ways; `key-from-text` refuses with a reason. |\n   447\t\n   448\tThe primitive behind it is a host library, never hand-rolled: `java.security`\n   449\t(`Ed25519`, JDK 17 and 21) on the JVM; `crypto.sign` and `crypto.verify`\n   450\twith `ed25519` keys on Node, required at run time so a browser build\n   451\tcompiles; a synchronous pure-Dart Ed25519 package on Dart, pinned in\n   452\t`pubspec.yaml`. An asynchronous-only package is not admissible. A host\n   453\twithout the primitive (a browser) refuses `sign-envelope` and\n   454\t`verify-envelope` is false: names do not resolve there, and direct addresses\n   455\tstill link.\n   456\t\n   457\t**Vectors.** `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn` is committed in L0 and\n   458\tevery host asserts it byte for byte:\n   459\t\n   460\t- RFC 8032 section 7.1 TEST 1, TEST 2 and TEST 3: seed, public key, message,\n   461\t  signature.\n   462\t- One assertion envelope and one retraction envelope under TEST 1's seed:\n   463\t  the envelope, its canonical bytes in hex, the signed message in hex, the\n   464\t  signature, and the envelope id.\n   465\t- Tamper cases, each `:bad-proof`: a changed name, manifest, sequence,\n   466\t  operation or principal; one flipped signature bit; another key; a\n   467\t  signature made without the prefix.\n   468\t\n   469\t### 6.5 The key file (owner decision 2)\n   470\t\n   471\tA publisher's key is loaded from a file named at startup\n   472\t(`--dht-key <file>`, or `:key` to `yin.vm.linker.dht` functions). The file is\n   473\tone EDN map:\n   474\t\n   475\t```clojure\n   476\t{:version 1\n   477\t :algorithm :ed25519\n   478\t :seed \"<64 lowercase hex>\"\n   479\t :public \"<64 lowercase hex>\"}\n   480\t```\n   481\t\n   482\t- `:public` must equal `(public-of seed)`; a mismatch, another version,\n   483\t  another algorithm, an extra key or a missing key refuses startup with the\n   484\t  reason. Nothing falls back to a fresh key.\n   485\t- A named file that does not exist refuses startup. A key is created only\n   486\t  by an explicit act: `--dht-keygen <file>` writes a new key file and exits,\n   487\t  and refuses to overwrite an existing file.\n   488\t- The file is written owner-readable only where the host can set that.\n   489\t- The seed is never printed, logged, written to the index, or sent. The\n   490\t  banner prints the principal.\n   491\t- Without a key a node publishes no name: `publish` refuses\n   492\t  `:yin.link.publish/no-key`. It still reads, resolves, loads and links.\n   493\t- A node with a key declares its own principal in its own authority, so it\n   494\t  resolves the names it published.\n   495\t- Tests use `(generate)` and hold the key in memory.\n   496\t\n   497\t**The sequence is derived.** A publisher's next `:yin.module/seq` is one\n   498\tmore than the greatest sequence among its own principal's envelopes in its\n   499\town index, and 1 when there are none. It is never stored beside the key.\n   500\t\n   501\t**Loss and replacement:**\n   502\t\n   503\t| Event | Consequence | Remedy |\n   504\t|---|---|---|\n   505\t| The key file is lost. | That principal can never sign again. Its assertions stand wherever a reader still declares it, and can never be retracted. | Generate a new key: a new principal. Publish again under it. Every reader replaces the old declaration with the new one. |\n   506\t| A reader declares both the old and the new principal. | A name both bind to the same manifest resolves (R8). A name they bind to different manifests is `:ambiguous-name`. | The reader removes the old declaration. |\n   507\t| The key is kept and the index directory is lost. | The derived sequence restarts at 1. Two distinct envelopes then share a sequence; a reader folding an old and a new snapshot sees equivocation and discards that principal from that sequence on. | Hydrate the directory from the last published index manifest before publishing, or use a new key. |\n   508\t| The key is disclosed. | Anyone holding it can bind names for that principal. Nothing in this epic revokes a key. | Every reader removes the declaration; the publisher takes a new key. |\n   509\t\n   510\t### 6.6 Republishing a name\n   511\t\n   512\tThe fold has no recency rule: two assertions by one principal for one name\n   513\twith different manifests are `:ambiguous-name`. Publishing a name its\n   514\tprincipal already binds to another manifest therefore writes, in one\n   515\ttransaction, a retraction of the standing assertion and the new assertion,\n   516\tat consecutive sequences. Publishing a name already bound to the same\n   517\tmanifest writes nothing.\n   518\t\n   519\t## 7. Resolution\n   520\t\n   521\t### 7.1 Authority is the reader's composition\n   522\t\n   523\t- The declared principals come from the reader's composition:\n   524\t  `--dht-principal <64 hex>` (repeatable), or `:authority` to\n   525\t  `yin.repl/create-state` and to the plain functions. They are never read\n   526\t  from an index.\n   527\t- An index manifest a reader chose to load is a **scope of considered\n   528\t  claims**. It authenticates nothing: content addressing proves which\n   529\t  snapshot was loaded; signatures and declarations decide whose claims\n   530\t  count.\n   531\t- ShiBi is a tuple space, and later it may supply declarations and write\n   532\t  capabilities as composition inputs. A tuple read from a candidate index\n   533\t  never establishes its own authority.\n   534\t\n   535\t### 7.2 The snapshot set\n   536\t\n   537\tA node's snapshot set is: the manifest its directory's HEAD names, when\n   538\tthere is one, and every manifest whose `:dao.space.dht/index` load is\n   539\t`:loaded` on that node. Nothing else in the local store is considered.\n   540\t\n   541\t- Loading an index is not a subscription. A publisher's later HEAD reaches a\n   542\t  reader only when its address is handed over and loaded.\n   543\t- The set is recorded as the sorted vector of its addresses. That vector is\n   544\t  the fold's `:snapshot` and appears in provenance.\n   545\t\n   546\t### 7.3 The fold\n   547\t\n   548\t```clojure\n   549\t(yin.vm.linker.dht/names node authority)\n   550\t;; -> authority/name-environment's answer:\n   551\t{:names {name entry} :diagnostics [...] :honored-seq {...} :snapshot [...]}\n   552\t```\n   553\t\n   554\t- For each snapshot, the envelope and proof datoms are read by query from\n   555\t  that index, and `authority/events-from-datoms` assembles them **per\n   556\t  snapshot**: an entity id is local to its index and proofs are joined only\n   557\t  within it.\n   558\t- The events of all snapshots are concatenated in snapshot order and folded\n   559\t  once by `authority/name-environment`. An envelope present in several\n   560\t  snapshots is one event (deduplication by content id).\n   561\t- Every declared principal's `:seq-floor` is 0 unless the composition\n   562\t  declares another.\n   563\t- One distinct manifest address resolves the name, whatever number of\n   564\t  declared principals assert it; provenance names them all (R8). More than\n   565\t  one distinct address is `:ambiguous-name` naming every address and\n   566\t  asserter. None is `:absent`.\n   567\t- The answer is a pure function of the snapshot vector and the authority.\n   568\t  A composition may keep it and recompute when either changes.\n   569\t- A direct entry (`:name-env {'my.lib address}`, `--link-name\n   570\t  my.lib=<address>`) is the composition as sole asserter. It wins over the\n   571\t  fold for that name and its provenance says `:composition`.\n   572\t\n   573\t### 7.4 Transitive requires\n   574\t\n   575\tA module's `:yin.module/requires` pins each dependency's manifest address,\n   576\tand discharge accepts only a linked module of that address. The closure\n   577\twalker loads the pinned closure. The install child still requires the\n   578\tdependency **by name**, so the reader's name environment must resolve that\n   579\tname to the pinned address; a different or absent resolution ends in\n   580\t`:unresolved-free`. A publisher that asserts a module asserts its\n   581\tdependencies in the same index.\n   582\t\n   583\t## 8. Evaluation\n   584\t\n   585\t### 8.1 Entry\n   586\t\n   587\tLoaded code enters a session by the flow of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2 and 7.3,\n   588\tunchanged: the require parks on the link pair; the interpreter answers under\n   589\tthe request's id; the receiving task correlates, discharges obligations\n   590\tagainst its own live state (step 5b), runs the install child, and lowers its\n   591\town bindings. This holds on all four VMs: each kernel names its format and\n   592\tcontract through `IModuleKernel/link-format`, and the `:fallback` policy of\n   593\t`yin.vm.linker.md` 5.5 applies as composed.\n   594\t\n   595\t### 8.2 Pending and the re-check (R11)\n   596\t\n   597\t- A link the DHT source answers pending records, on the shell's pending\n   598\t  run, the module manifest address whose load it waits on.\n   599\t- `yin.repl.main/step-all` calls `yin.repl/recheck-pending` once in a tick\n   600\t  if and only if that tick's node events hold a `:loaded` or `:load-failed`\n   601\t  event whose `:manifest` is one a pending link waits on.\n   602\t- Any other node event (a bind, a publication settling, an index load,\n   603\t  another module's load) triggers no re-check and changes no `:checks`.\n   604\t- A terminal load event arrives once. A re-check after it finds the record\n   605\t  terminal and answers the link; a second re-check for the same address\n   606\t  cannot happen without a new load.\n   607\t- One re-check serves every pending request in order, so several pending\n   608\t  module loads completing in one tick need one re-check.\n   609\t- A typed line still re-checks, as today. `(abandon)`, the retained lines,\n   610\t  the identity carry and the late-answer skip of M5 are unchanged.\n   611\t- `:link-policy` is consulted exactly as `yin.repl.link-policy.md` 3.2\n   612\t  says. `:manual` stays the default.\n   613\t\n   614\t### 8.3 A request budget is not a lease (R12)\n   615\t\n   616\tA `:jing/get` ends within the DHT's `get-ticks` or when its candidates are\n   617\texhausted. That bounds **one fetch**. It does not bound the pending run: a\n   618\tclosure is many fetches, a failed load may be started again by a new\n   619\trequire, and a link waiting on a load that never starts is not bounded at\n   620\tall. Only a `:link-policy` ends a pending run without `(abandon)`. The\n   621\t`:lease` policy of `yin.repl.link-policy.md` section 6 remains the designed\n   622\tdeadline and remains deferred.\n   623\t\n   624\t### 8.4 Implicit network (R10)\n   625\t\n   626\tUnder a DHT link source, `(require 'x)` for a name that resolves starts a\n   627\tload. The startup banner and `(help)` state it: a require may fetch code\n   628\tfrom peers. A solo node starts the load too and fails it on the node's next\n   629\tstep with cause `/solo`.\n   630\t\n   631\t## 9. Failure vocabulary\n   632\t\n   633\tEvery failure is plain data on the link response, raised to the program as\n   634\tthe require's error, and printed by the REPL from that data.\n   635\t\n   636\t| Outcome | Carried data | When |\n   637\t|---|---|---|\n   638\t| `:absent` (name) | `:name`, `:diagnostics` for that name | The fold found no accepted assertion. Diagnostics name every discarded envelope for the name: `:unauthenticated` (`:no-proof`, `:bad-proof`), `:undeclared-principal`, `:replay`, `:equivocation`, `:dangling-retraction`, `:malformed-envelope`. |\n   639\t| `:ambiguous-name` | `:addresses`, `:asserters` | More than one distinct address. |\n   640\t| `:absent` (content) | `:address`, `:cause` a section 4.3 `:miss` reason | The load failed for want of a blob. |\n   641\t| `:descriptor-defect` | `:address`, `:role`, `:defect` | The load's walk answered `:invalid`. |\n   642\t| `:yin.link.dht/closure-incomplete` | `:address` | A link refused `:absent` after a `:loaded` record. A defect, reported. |\n   643\t| The linker's own refusals | per `yin.vm.linker/refusal-reasons` | `:contract-mismatch`, `:module-name-mismatch`, `:derivation-mismatch`, `:unverified-derivation`, `:undeclared-free`, `:use-before-definition`, `:unresolved-free`, `:shadowed-free`, `:unsupported-format`, and the rest, unchanged. |\n   644\t| `:yin.repl/abandoned`, `:yin.repl/link-policy` | per the link policy | The run was abandoned. |\n   645\t\n   646\tPublication refusals, returned by the plain functions and printed by the\n   647\tREPL: `:yin.link.publish/no-key`, `/undefined-export`, `/undeclared-free`,\n   648\t`/host-module`, and the DHT's `/unacknowledged` reasons through the\n   649\t`:published` event.\n   650\t\n   651\t## 10. The plain Clojure API\n   652\t\n   653\tThe one path, for any Clojure program:\n   654\t\n   655\t```text\n   656\tjoin -> load-index -> names -> load-module -> link        (reader)\n   657\tjoin -> publish!  -> assert! -> announce!                 (publisher)\n   658\t```\n   659\t\n   660\t| Function | Meaning |\n   661\t|---|---|\n   662\t| `dao.space.dht/join`, `step`, `store`, `local`, `announce!`, `close!` | S5, unchanged. |\n   663\t| `dao.space.dht/load-index`, `load-status`, `db`, `q` | S5; statuses and reasons per section 4.3. |\n   664\t| `dao.space.dht/load`, `forget` | Section 4.3. |\n   665\t| `yin.vm.linker.closure/walk` | Section 4.2. |\n   666\t| `yin.vm.linker.sign/*` | Section 6.4. |\n   667\t| `yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!`, `module-from-index`, `assertion`, `retraction` | Sections 5.2, 5.3, 6.1. `assertion` and `retraction` answer `{:envelope e :proof p :datoms [...]}` for a key and a sequence. |\n   668\t| `(yin.vm.linker.dht/publish! node spec)` | 5.4. |\n   669\t| `(yin.vm.linker.dht/snapshots node)` | The snapshot vector of 7.2. |\n   670\t| `(yin.vm.linker.dht/names node authority)` | 7.3. |\n   671\t| `(yin.vm.linker.dht/load-module node manifest-address)` | Starts the closure load; answers the node. |\n   672\t| `(yin.vm.linker.dht/module-status node manifest-address)` | The load status of 4.3. |\n   673\t| `(yin.vm.linker.dht/link node manifest-address format opts)` | `link-manifest` over the node's local store; refused `:yin.link.dht/not-loaded` unless the load is `:loaded`. |\n   674\t\n   675\t`yin.vm.linker.dht` depends on `dao.space.dht` and `yin.vm.linker.*`, and on\n   676\tnothing under `yin.repl`.\n   677\t\n   678\t**The interface gate.** `yin.repl` reaches every operation above through\n   679\tthese functions:\n   680\t\n   681\t- `(require 'name)` is served by `yin.repl.link` calling `names`,\n   682\t  `load-module`, `module-status` and `local-runtime` + `link-manifest`.\n   683\t- The `dao.space.dht` host module gains `load-module` and `module-status`,\n   684\t  beside `load-index`, `load-status` and `q`, as effects on the query call\n   685\t  pair.\n   686\t- A `yin.link` host module answers `(yin.link/publish 'name '[exports])` and\n   687\t  `(yin.link/names)`.\n   688\t\n   689\tA host-module answer function contains argument checking, one call into the\n   690\tplain API, and the response. It contains no walk, no fetch loop, no fold and\n   691\tno signing. A reviewer refuses a slice whose REPL namespaces implement any\n   692\tof those.\n   693\t\n   694\t## 11. Limits and defaults\n   695\t\n   696\t| Datum | Default | Notes |\n   697\t|---|---|---|\n   698\t| Closure walk bounds | `yin.vm.linker/default-bounds` | Parts, depth and bytes, as the linker's own. |\n   699\t| Outstanding fetches per load | 1 | As `load-index` today. |\n   700\t| Replicate backlog outstanding | the DHT's `max-pending-writes` (64) | Section 5.1. |\n   701\t| `:seq-floor` | 0 | Per declared principal. |\n   702\t| Link attempt budget | 64 drive rounds | `yin.repl.link/attempt-budget`, unchanged. |\n   703\t| Name scope | HEAD and loaded index manifests | Section 7.2. |\n   704\t\n   705\t## 12. Slices\n   706\t\n   707\tOrder: **L0 ∥ L1 → L2 → L3 → L4 → L5.** L0 and L1 own disjoint files. No\n   708\tslice before L3 touches `yin/repl/*` sources.\n   709\t\n   710\t**L0 — publisher, signing, local runtime.** Plain Clojure, host neutral.\n   711\tFiles: `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc` (`local-runtime`), new\n   712\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc`, new\n   713\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc`, `pubspec.yaml` (the Dart Ed25519\n   714\tpackage), `test/yin/repl/require_test.cljc`,\n   715\t`test/yin/vm/linker_manifest_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc`, new\n   716\t`test/yin/vm/linker/sign_test.cljc`, new\n   717\t`test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn`, new\n   718\t`test/yin/vm/linker/publish_test.cljc`. Acceptance:\n   719\t- The vectors of 6.4 pass byte for byte on the JVM, Node and Dart, the RFC\n   720\t  8032 vectors included.\n   721\t- Tampering with the envelope's name, manifest, sequence, operation or\n   722\t  principal, with the proof, or with the key fails `:bad-proof` through\n   723\t  `authority/name-environment`.\n   724\t- `key-from-text` refuses each malformed file of 6.5 with its reason.\n   725\t- `publish-module!` replaces both test helpers; the existing require and\n   726\t  manifest tests pass unchanged against it.\n   727\t- `:links` reports ok on all four formats for the closed corpus, and the\n   728\t  tree and semantic refusals beside stack and register ok for the store\n   729\t  corpus.\n   730\t- An undefined export writes nothing.\n   731\t- `yin.repl.link` and the tests share `local-runtime`; no behaviour changes.\n   732\t\n   733\t**L1 — the staged load.** Files: `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc`,\n   734\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc` (reason rendering only),\n   735\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (status shape only),\n   736\t`test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc`, `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`. Acceptance:\n   737\t- `load-index` is `load`; every existing index-load test passes with\n   738\t  reasons asserted as data.\n   739\t- A walk answering `:missing` fetches; `:complete` loads; `:invalid` fails\n   740\t  without any fetch; a throwing walk is `:invalid`.\n   741\t- Each of `/exhausted`, `/deadline`, `/solo` and `/busy` reaches the\n   742\t  `:failed` reason as `:cause`.\n   743\t- A peer serving bytes that do not hash to the address never produces\n   744\t  `:loaded`; the blob is not in `:local`.\n   745\t- Exactly one terminal event per load. `forget` clears a terminal record\n   746\t  and is refused while loading.\n   747\t- Time advances only by appended ticks.\n   748\t\n   749\t**L2 — the closure walker and module load, plain Clojure, by address.**\n   750\tFiles: new `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc`, new\n   751\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc`, new\n   752\t`test/yin/vm/linker/closure_test.cljc`, new\n   753\t`test/yin/vm/linker/dht_test.cljc` (over `test/dao/jing/dht/mesh.cljc`).\n   754\tAcceptance:\n   755\t- With the closure otherwise complete, removing in turn the manifest, a\n   756\t  leaf row, an interior row, each of the three derivation records, each of\n   757\t  the three lowered images, and a blob of a transitively required module\n   758\t  yields `:missing` naming exactly that address and role.\n   759\t- A corrupt manifest, a record leading from another tree, an H with no index\n   760\t  entry, and an image that fails its identity each yield `:invalid` and the\n   761\t  load fails without retry.\n   762\t- Two mesh nodes: A publishes and announces, acknowledged; B loads by\n   763\t  address; then **links on all four formats while the node's request ring\n   764\t  records zero `:jing/get`**, and each result is B0-equal to A's local run.\n   765\t- Linking one format never makes a load `:loaded`: the load is `:loaded`\n   766\t  only on the whole closure.\n   767\t- A missing image no peer holds ends `:failed` with the miss cause; A with\n   768\t  `publish?` false ends B's load `/exhausted`.\n   769\t- `link` before `:loaded` is `:yin.link.dht/not-loaded`.\n   770\t\n   771\t**L3 — the REPL's DHT link source and the relevant re-check.** Files:\n   772\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`,\n   773\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc`,\n   774\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc`, `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`,\n   775\t`test/yin/repl/require_test.cljc`. Names are direct addresses in this\n   776\tslice. Acceptance:\n   777\t- A require whose module a peer holds parks, and completes on a later tick\n   778\t  **with no typed line**; the export then answers.\n   779\t- An unrelated node event (a publication settling, an index load, another\n   780\t  module's load) causes no re-check and leaves `:checks` unchanged.\n   781\t- A repeated or late event for an answered link changes nothing.\n   782\t- A function policy that abandons at N checks counts only re-checks of this\n   783\t  run; `:keep` never ends it.\n   784\t- `(abandon)` during a load ends the require; the load's later completion\n   785\t  settles no later require; a new require of the same name finds the\n   786\t  closure `:loaded` and links.\n   787\t- A failed load raises the section 9 refusal, and a new require starts a\n   788\t  new load.\n   789\t- A solo node's require fails with cause `/solo` after the node's next\n   790\t  step.\n   791\t- All four VMs: the closed corpus links and answers; a failed load raises\n   792\t  the same refusal on each; the register fallback policy behaves as\n   793\t  composed.\n   794\t- `(dao.space.dht/load-module m)` and `module-status` answer through the\n   795\t  plain functions.\n   796\t- The banner and `(help)` state that a require may fetch from peers.\n   797\t\n   798\t**L4 — rows every round, names in the index, publish at the prompt.**\n   799\tFiles: `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc`, `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc`\n   800\t(replicate backlog), `src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (the `yin.link` host\n   801\tmodule), `src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`,\n   802\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc` (flags, banner, keygen),\n   803\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc` (`module-from-index`),\n   804\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc` (`snapshots`, `names`), the tests beside\n   805\teach, and the design docs this one cross-references. Acceptance:\n   806\t- After a round, every row of the evaluated program is in the index store,\n   807\t  and a second node loads that program's tree by its `:yin.repl/root`\n   808\t  address.\n   809\t- A program of more rows than the DHT's pending-write bound is published\n   810\t  acknowledged, never `/busy`.\n   811\t- `(yin.link/publish 'my.lib '[f])` over two defining programs, one free\n   812\t  primitive and one linker-required module produces one tree in `t` order\n   813\t  and a manifest declaring both; an undefined export, an undeclared free\n   814\t  name, a host-module name and a missing key each refuse and write nothing.\n   815\t- Republishing a name with a new manifest writes the retraction and the\n   816\t  assertion; a reader of the new snapshot resolves the new address; a\n   817\t  reader holding only the old snapshot resolves the old one.\n   818\t- The fold over the snapshot set: an undeclared principal and a bad\n   819\t  signature are reported and do not resolve; two declared principals on\n   820\t  different addresses refuse `:ambiguous-name` naming both; two on the same\n   821\t  address resolve with both in provenance; a retraction removes exactly its\n   822\t  assertion; an envelope present in two snapshots counts once.\n   823\t- Only HEAD and loaded index manifests are folded: an envelope blob that is\n   824\t  merely in the local store is never considered.\n   825\t- The key file cases of 6.5: absent, malformed, mismatched public key,\n   826\t  keygen refusing to overwrite. The seed appears in no output.\n   827\t- The next sequence is derived from the index after a restart.\n   828\t\n   829\t**L5 — end to end.** Files: `test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`, new\n   830\t`test/yin/vm/linker/dht_end_to_end_test.cljc`,\n   831\t`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`. Acceptance:\n   832\t- Process A (`dht:<dir>`, a peer, `--dht-publish`, `--dht-key`) defines a\n   833\t  function and publishes it as a module. The test reads A's index manifest\n   834\t  address and principal from A's output.\n   835\t- Process B (`--dht-manifest`, `--dht-principal`) requires the module by\n   836\t  name, waits through pending, and evaluates the export, on each of the\n   837\t  four VMs. B is run on the JVM and on Node.\n   838\t- Plain Clojure does the same through\n   839\t  `join → load-index → names → load-module → link`, the very functions the\n   840\t  host module calls, and runs the image; it also asserts each failure\n   841\t  result of section 9 as data.\n   842\t- Without `--dht-principal`, B's require is `:absent` with an\n   843\t  `:undeclared-principal` diagnostic.\n   844\t- The in-process form of the same scenario passes on Dart over the mesh\n   845\t  seam, signatures verified, on all four VMs.\n   846\t- The store corpus (an export reading a module-level definition) evaluates\n   847\t  on the stack and register VMs and refuses on the other two with the\n   848\t  linker's reason.\n   849\t\n   850\t## 13. Deferrals\n   851\t\n   852\t- **The persistent stepped linker.** `link-manifest` as a state machine the\n   853\t  ticker advances beside the node, with the node's rings as the content\n   854\t  pair and no prefetch. The staged load is the interim implementation, and\n   855\t  its acceptance gate is L2's zero-fetch link.\n   856\t- **Latest-root discovery and rendezvous.** A reader is handed every index\n   857\t  manifest address it loads.\n   858\t- **The `:lease` link policy.** `yin.repl.link-policy.md` section 6.\n   859\t- **Key rotation and revocation.** A rotation or revocation envelope under\n   860\t  the same fold is the candidate (`yin.vm.linker.md` section 12).\n   861\t- **Resolving a transitive require by its pinned address**, and delivery of\n   862\t  a dependency closure as one response (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.4).\n   863\t- **Step 5a for module-level reads in the tree formats.** Which modules\n   864\t  link on the walker and semantic VMs is the linker's rule, not this\n   865\t  epic's.\n   866\t- **Host modules as dependencies of a published module.**\n   867\t- **Availability repair.** A row whose replication was never acknowledged\n   868\t  is not sent again once its publication is reported; pinning, repair and\n   869\t  garbage collection are `dao.jing.dht.md`'s deferrals.\n   870\t- **A node id derived from the publisher's key** (`dao.jing.dht.md` 6).\n   871\t- **ShiBi.** Declarations and write capabilities as tuples supplied to the\n   872\t  composition.\n   873\t- **Signed names in a browser build.**\n   874\t\n   875\t## Lineage\n   876\t\n   877\tThe split is Datomic's and Unison's at once: facts about code are datoms a\n   878\tquery reads, and code to run is content a hash names. The name layer is the\n   879\tpetname idea made fail-closed: a key is its own name, a reader chooses whose\n   880\tclaims count, and two claims that disagree resolve nothing.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat -n '/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat -n '/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tDesign pass complete. The full findings are below and are saved at `/Users/sto/.claude/plans/read-collab-1790806000000-architect-link-iridescent-snail.md`. Plan mode blocked writing into `collab/`, so the findings file was not staged there.\n     2\t\n     3\t# Architect findings: load and evaluate code from dao.jing.dht via the linker\n     4\t\n     5\tRead-only pass on master c66809fa. This is a lead design, not a sign-off. A second opinion follows.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What the tree holds today\n     8\t\n     9\tThese facts shape every ruling below.\n    10\t\n    11\t- **The linker has two content kinds.** A `dao.jing` handle served in process over ring buffers, or a remote content-pair client. One attempt runs the whole manifest link synchronously against a fresh link state and fresh cursors for at most 64 drive rounds, then reports pending. The remote drive advances nothing but a counter. See `src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:106-227`.\n    12\t- **The DHT node reads local only and stages misses around the handle.** The index loader walks against a probing handle, fetches each missing blob with a content request, and repeats. See `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:457-597`. This is the mechanism dao.jing.dht.md section 5.1 prescribes.\n    13\t- **No REPL store holds any program's rows.** The tree materializer has no caller in `src/`. The index holds AST datoms whose address facts point at row addresses that exist nowhere. A manifest's tree address would be absent from every REPL store today.\n    14\t- **A REPL definition is an application of the variable `yin/def` to a literal symbol.** Definitions are derivable by query. No definition-name fact exists and none is needed.\n    15\t- **The only manifest publisher is a test helper**, duplicated in two test namespaces. Nothing in `src/` mints a manifest.\n    16\t- **The authority policy is implemented and wired into nothing.** The fold over signed envelope datoms exists in `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc`. The REPL takes the name environment as a plain map.\n    17\t- **No host driver re-checks a pending run.** The shell's re-check step exists at `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1848`, but the ticker never calls it. A parked require progresses only when the user types a line. This settles the open question in the link-policy doc, section 4: the drivers cannot.\n    18\t- **The host-function pattern is settled.** The REPL's `dao.space.dht` module already threads the node through the query serve and answers load and query calls as effects on the call pair.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 1. Content source\n    21\t\n    22\t**Ruling.** The node from `dao.space.dht/join` is the linker's content source, composed as the local kind over the node's own store handle. The manifest's closure is loaded onto the node before the link, by the same staged-miss mechanism the index loader uses.\n    23\t\n    24\tA content client over the node's request and answer rings is admissible by shape, since those rings speak the plain content convention. It cannot work with the REPL's link interpreter as written. Each attempt mints a fresh state at the newest cursor, so an answer the DHT produces on a later tick lands for a state that no longer exists. The remote drive also cannot step the node, which has one step owner and a clock reading the drive lacks. Fixing that means rewriting the manifest link flow as a persistent stepped state machine the ticker advances beside the node. That is the right end state and it stays deferred, because it changes the linker core rather than the composition.\n    25\t\n    26\tThe staged path needs no linker change:\n    27\t\n    28\t1. Generalize the index loader into a generic load that takes a walk function over a probing handle. The index loader becomes one call of it. The dao namespace stays ignorant of yin.\n    29\t2. The module walk is the linker itself. Linking over a probing local runtime throws the missing signal on the first absent blob, the loader fetches it, and the walk repeats. Every blob the link touches, transitive requires included, is discovered by walking and listed nowhere. That is derive, don't persist.\n    30\t3. After the load reports loaded, the REPL's answer step links from the local store in one attempt. Before that, it starts the load and reports pending.\n    31\t\n    32\tThe REPL's node is one value shared by the query host module and the link source. Both thread it through their serve. A plain Clojure program holds the same node and calls the same functions. The node's busy flag already keeps the ticker at base cadence during loads.\n    33\t\n    34\tThe local runtime helper exists twice in tests and once inside the REPL. It moves into `src` once.\n    35\t\n    36\t## 2. What gets published\n    37\t\n    38\t**Ruling.** Both, and they are different kinds of thing. The index is facts. The linkable module is content: rows, images, derivation records, manifest. The name binding is one envelope datom pair in the index. No new datom family is added for the module image.\n    39\t\n    40\tAgainst derive-don't-persist:\n    41\t\n    42\t- The tree is derivable from the index and the lowered images are derivable from the tree. But the linker's contract is content fetched by address and verified under the algorithm the address carries. Re-deriving a row on each content request would make the content server structure-aware and re-encode on every fetch. So rows and images are materialized as content. They are content-addressed and idempotent.\n    43\t- The manifest and derivation records are the publisher's claims and the ledger's record of a lowering. No query can derive them. They are persisted as the linker design already specifies.\n    44\t- The name binding is persisted as exactly one thing: the section 8.2 assertion envelope and its proof, as datoms in the code index. The name environment is derived from those datoms at each snapshot.\n    45\t- The H and R index stays inside the manifest and is merged link-locally, as today.\n    46\t\n    47\t**Recommendation, owner decision 1.** Materialize every evaluated program's rows into the index store each round. The cost is one content write per row. The benefit is that the index's address facts finally name real content, and any evaluated program becomes linkable by tree address without a manifest. The publish banner already says the whole code index is shared.\n    48\t\n    49\t## 3. Naming and discovery\n    50\t\n    51\t**Ruling.** The index-derived name environment, with the authority policy as the derivation. A published name-environment root is rejected. Direct addresses stay as the zero-cost escape hatch.\n    52\t\n    53\t- **Index-derived.** Publishing a module transacts one signed assertion envelope into the session's index as one transaction. The covered index the DHT already replicates carries it. A reader that loads an index derives its name environment by querying the envelope and proof datoms, feeding them to the existing ingestion and fold, with the index manifest address as the snapshot. The only address handed out of band is the one S5 already hands over.\n    54\t- **A published root** would be a second thing to discover for facts the index already holds. Rejected.\n    55\t- **Direct addresses** remain: a plain name map, plus a flag binding a name to a manifest address. That is the composition as sole asserter, the first shape in section 8.2.\n    56\t\n    57\tThe minimal honest step: a reader learns names from the indexes it has loaded. A name published after the reader loaded is invisible until the reader loads the publisher's newer index manifest, by address, out of band. That is S5's contract and this pass does not widen it. Latest-root discovery, key rotation and revocation stay deferred.\n    58\t\n    59\t**Proof kind is signatures, not attested logs.** The DHT erases carriers by design, so an attested proof dies the moment an envelope leaves its log. A signature over the envelope's canonical bytes survives any carrier. The verify function is composition-supplied host code, as the authority namespace already assumes. Ed25519 is in the JDK and in Node's crypto module. Dart needs a package, which is owner decision 2.\n    60\t\n    61\t## 4. Authority and trust\n    62\t\n    63\t- **Who may bind a name.** A principal the reader declares, with a proof that verifies. An undeclared principal's envelope is discarded. The declaration is composition data, from a flag or the create-state map, and is never read from the index it authenticates.\n    64\t- **What stops shadowing.** Two layers. A peer cannot write into another node's index, because the DHT replicates content by address and the reader chose which manifests to load. Inside the fold, two manifests for one name from declared principals refuse as ambiguous with every address and asserter named, and no tie-break exists. A name is shadowed only by a principal the reader itself declared, and then the reader is told rather than served.\n    65\t- **What trust reduces to.** Under verifying derivation, the default, the receiver re-lowers the tree and checks the name, contracts, derivations and every image by content. The single trusted claim is that principal P asserted name N resolves to manifest M.\n    66\t- **ShiBi.** As a tuple space, ShiBi arrives later in two places: the authority declarations a workspace admits can become tuples a composition queries, and write capability into a shared index is where authorization lands, as the publication section of the DHT doc already says. Now, the declared principal set must remain composition data. Deriving the root of trust from the index it authenticates would verify a claim against a key the same claim supplied.\n    67\t\n    68\t## 5. Evaluation semantics\n    69\t\n    70\t**Entry into a session** is unchanged from the linker design. A require parks on the link pair, the interpreter resolves the name and links from the node's local store with deferred discharge, the response correlates by id, step 5b discharges against the receiving task's live state, the install child runs, and each task lowers its own bindings. The only new branch is in the answer step: with a DHT source, a closure not yet local starts a load and answers pending, and a failed load answers absent with the load's reason as its cause.\n    71\t\n    72\t**Link policy and pending.** Manual stays the default. A pending require now has a progress source that is not a typed line, so the host ticker must call the shell's re-check after a tick in which the node reported a load event. The check counter already resets on progress, so function policies behave.\n    73\t\n    74\t**Lease** stays deferred. The DHT already gives a data-visible deadline: a get that exhausts candidates or its tick budget ends in a miss fact, the load fails with that reason, the link is refused, and the require's error carries it. No clock enters the REPL.\n    75\t\n    76\t**Across the four VMs.** Each kernel names its format and contract through the module kernel protocol. The manifest carries the tree and three derivation records, so publish must mint all four images. The H-for-R fallback still applies. Under verifying derivation the tree rows must be local for every lowered format, which the walk guarantees. The existing B0-equality test extends to a manifest loaded from a peer.\n    77\t\n    78\t**Failure as data.** The link response carries the linker's closed refusal vocabulary, the fold's ambiguous and absent outcomes with diagnostics, and absent with the DHT's miss reasons from a failed load. Unauthenticated and undeclared-principal diagnostics travel beside absent so the REPL can print why a name visibly present in the index did not resolve. Not found from a DHT is not authoritative absence, so a refused require can be retried, and the identity carry from M5 keeps a late answer from settling a later require.\n    79\t\n    80\t## 6. Slices\n    81\t\n    82\tOrder: L0 and L1 in parallel, then L2, L3, L4, L5.\n    83\t\n    84\t**L0. Publisher and runtime in src, plain Clojure.**\n    85\t- Promote the local runtime into the linker namespace.\n    86\t- New `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc`: the test publisher promoted, plus assertion and retraction envelope minting with a sign seam.\n    87\t- An Ed25519 host seam for keygen, sign and verify over canonical bytes.\n    88\t- Acceptance: both existing manifest test namespaces use the src publisher unchanged. A signed envelope verifies through the fold on every host with the seam. A tampered byte fails as bad proof.\n    89\t\n    90\t**L1. Generic staged load in dao.space.dht.**\n    91\t- A load taking a walk function. The index loader reimplemented over it. Status and events unchanged, with a kind added.\n    92\t- Acceptance: existing DHT tests pass unchanged. A walk over an arbitrary blob closure across two mesh nodes fetches every missing blob. Every miss reason maps to a load-failed reason.\n    93\t\n    94\t**L2. Module load and link over the DHT, plain Clojure end to end by address.**\n    95\t- New `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc`, depending on the DHT and linker namespaces, not on the REPL: load a module, read its status, link after loaded, and derive a name environment from loaded indexes.\n    96\t- Acceptance over the mesh seam: A publishes and announces, acknowledged. B loads by address, links on all four formats under verifying derivation, and runs equal to local. A missing image on every peer fails absent with the miss reason. A swapped record fails as derivation mismatch. A non-publishing A makes B's load exhaust.\n    97\t\n    98\t**L3. REPL: DHT link source, pending, re-check.**\n    99\t- The link composition gains a DHT kind chosen when the index store is a DHT store. The answer step starts loads. The serve takes and returns the node as the query serve does.\n   100\t- The ticker calls the shell's re-check after node events.\n   101\t- The DHT host module gains load-module and module-status calls.\n   102\t- Acceptance: with a DHT store and a direct name map, a require on a reader whose peer holds the module parks, prints a loading line, completes on a later tick without a typed line, and the function answers. Link-policy tests still hold. A solo node refuses absent at once.\n   103\t\n   104\t**L4. Names in the index: publish and derive.**\n   105\t- The indexer commits envelope datoms as a second packet kind and materializes rows per round, per owner decision 1.\n   106\t- A REPL publish host function builds the module tree from the index's defining programs, publishes on the node store, transacts the signed assertion, announces, and prints the manifest address.\n   107\t- Startup flags declare principals and load or mint the process keypair. The banner prints the public key beside what the node shares.\n   108\t- The link source's name environment becomes a function of the loaded index snapshots, rebuilt at each serve, with fold diagnostics printed once per change.\n   109\t- Acceptance: B hydrates A's index and resolves the name with provenance naming A. An undeclared principal is reported and not resolved. Two declared principals on one name print ambiguous with both. A retraction removes exactly its assertion.\n   110\t\n   111\t**L5. End to end across processes**, extending the existing process test.\n   112\t- Process A publishes a function as a module over the anchor. Process B, on the JVM and the Node leg, hydrates A's index, declares A's key, requires the module, waits through pending, and evaluates it on the semantic and stack VMs.\n   113\t- Plain Clojure does the same through join, load-index, the derived name environment, load-module and link, then runs the image, asserting equality with A.\n   114\t\n   115\t## 7. Owner decisions\n   116\t\n   117\t1. **Rows per round.** Materialize every evaluated program's rows each round, recommended, or only at explicit publish.\n   118\t2. **Signatures from day one, and Dart.** Add an Ed25519 package for Dart, recommended, or let Dart readers use direct addresses until then. Inventing an unproven trusted-index mode would be a third trust policy and is not recommended.\n   119\t3. **Module granularity and spelling.** Explicit publish of named exports deriving the tree from the index, recommended, versus publishing a source form, versus auto-publishing every definition.\n   120\t4. **Implicit network on require.** A require under a DHT store starts a load without further consent, recommended and stated in the banner, or requires an explicit load first.\n   121\t5. **Name environment scope.** Fold over every loaded index, recommended, or only the hydrated HEAD's.\n   122\t6. **Key material.** A keypair minted per process and printed, or loaded from a file across restarts. Rotation and revocation stay open.\n   123\t7. **Confirm the deferrals.** The persistent stepped linker over the node's rings, latest-root discovery, and the lease link policy are not built in this epic.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat -n '/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht-second-opinion.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat -n '/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht-second-opinion.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tThe core claims check out so far: the link attempt restarts with fresh cursors, and `dao.space.dht` stages index misses through the node’s local handle. I also found a likely acceptance gap: the proposed module walk needs to prove it fetches everything verifying derivation will read, not just the image selected by one link request.\n     2\t# Architect second opinion\n     3\t\n     4\t**Overall: AGREE-WITH-CHANGES.** The lead identifies the right composition boundary, but the proposed staged load has an unproved closure walk, the authority policy is weaker than the prose claims, and several slice acceptances would pass without exercising the property they are meant to establish. This is a read-only review of `c66809fa`; I did not run tests or change files.\n     5\t\n     6\t## What the tree holds today\n     7\t\n     8\t| Lead claim | Verification |\n     9\t|---|---|\n    10\t| A link attempt starts fresh and a remote drive cannot advance the node. | **Confirmed.** Each attempt creates link state and new local cursors; the remote drive only increments its round counter. An exhausted attempt returns pending. [link.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:167), [link.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:198), [link.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:206). |\n    11\t| DHT index loading stages misses into the node’s local store. | **Confirmed.** The probing handle signals a missing address, `advance-load` requests it through the node client, then repeats the index walk. [dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:457), [dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:539). |\n    12\t| REPL code index address facts do not mean the program tree rows are stored. | **Confirmed in the current path.** Projection writes `:yin/address` facts; the round’s materialization drains the *index publication* intake. These are distinct from `yin.vm/materialize-tree!`, used by linker tests. [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:93), [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:300), [linker_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc:325). |\n    13\t| Definition names can be queried without a new fact. | **Confirmed.** The current AST projection records the tree, and the query test locates `yin/def` through its operator and literal operand. [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:102), [query_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:541). |\n    14\t| There is no production manifest publisher. | **Confirmed with a qualification.** `linker/publish!` stores an individual format image; manifest assembly is in test helpers, not a production module publisher. [linker.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:1992), [linker_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc:325). |\n    15\t| Authority fold is not wired into REPL resolution. | **Confirmed.** The fold exists, while the REPL composition receives a plain `name-env` map and looks names up directly. [authority.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc:258), [link.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:106), [link.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:230). |\n    16\t| No host ticker re-checks a pending run. | **Confirmed.** `recheck-pending` exists, but `step-all` advances DHT and driver without calling it. [repl.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1848), [main.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc:286). |\n    17\t\n    18\tThe lead’s statement that **two declared principals always cause ambiguity is incorrect**. The fold returns success when multiple accepted assertions name the *same address*, retaining both asserters in provenance. It refuses only when there is more than one distinct address. [authority.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc:226).\n    19\t\n    20\t## 1. Content source — **AGREE-WITH-CHANGES**\n    21\t\n    22\tUsing the node from `dao.space.dht/join` and its local handle is a sound first composition. A direct content client over the node’s rings is not viable with today’s REPL attempt lifecycle: late answers can outlive the fresh state and cursors that requested them. Deferring a persistent stepped link coordinator is reasonable.\n    23\t\n    24\tThe proposed “run the linker against a probing handle” needs a proof before L1/L2 are called complete. The index walker exposes missing blobs by throwing; the module linker may instead turn a missing content answer into a normal `:absent` refusal. Also, linking one selected format does not necessarily walk every image needed by another format or by transitive requirements. Define a module closure walker with explicit outcomes—**missing address, verified complete, invalid content**—and test a missing blob at every level. Keep the DHT node as the sole step owner; do not have a linker drive it internally.\n    25\t\n    26\t## 2. Published content — **AGREE-WITH-CHANGES**\n    27\t\n    28\tPublish an index for query and a module content closure for linking. Content-addressed tree rows and lowered images are valid *materialized outputs* of a derivation; they are not extra canonical facts asserting a second meaning for the AST. The manifest and derivation records carry publication claims that a query over code facts cannot supply. This fits the scoped “derive, don’t persist” rule, which specifically warns against adding derivable tags, slots, or fields to canonical structures. [datom.world.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:151).\n    29\t\n    30\tI **disagree** with materializing every evaluated program’s rows every round. It adds storage and a public content consequence to ordinary evaluation, while the proposed user operation is explicit module publication. Materialize the chosen module tree and closure at publish time. If `:yin/address` in the index promises a retrievable row today, either fulfill that separate index contract deliberately or document its current meaning; do not use the linker epic to silently broaden every evaluation’s publication.\n    31\t\n    32\t## 3. Naming and discovery — **AGREE-WITH-CHANGES**\n    33\t\n    34\tDeriving names from signed envelope and proof datoms in an explicitly loaded index snapshot is the smallest honest discovery step. It preserves the existing out-of-band index-manifest handoff. The fold already assembles envelope/proof pairs and accepts a composition-supplied verifier. [authority.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc:63), [authority.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc:258).\n    35\t\n    36\tAn attested-carrier proof does not survive this transport: the index read is not the original stream identity that attested the assertion. Require a portable signature for DHT-discovered names. Specify canonical signed bytes, key encoding, principal identifier, and cross-host test vectors before selecting host crypto adapters. Ed25519 is a plausible choice, but “JDK and Node have it” is not yet a complete JVM/Node/Dart seam. Direct manifest addresses should remain available without a signature-based name claim.\n    37\t\n    38\tDo not imply that loading an index means subscribing to its future HEAD. A new snapshot still requires its address to be handed to the reader.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 4. Authority — **AGREE-WITH-CHANGES**\n    41\t\n    42\tDeclared principals must come from reader composition, never from the index they authenticate. The current fold correctly discards undeclared principals and failed proofs. [authority.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc:283). Keep ambiguity refusal for *different manifest addresses*; explicitly accept or change the current same-address consensus rule. Either way, diagnostics must retain all asserting principals.\n    43\t\n    44\tA chosen index manifest is a **scope of considered claims**, not a write-authentication guarantee. Content addressing proves which snapshot was loaded; signatures and local declarations decide whose name claims count. ShiBi may later supply declarations or write capabilities as composition inputs, but a tuple read from the candidate index cannot establish its own authority.\n    45\t\n    46\t## 5. Evaluation entry — **AGREE-WITH-CHANGES**\n    47\t\n    48\tKeep the existing link request and deferred discharge path for each receiving VM. The four format records are already supplied to REPL link state. [link.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:70), [link.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:184). DHT loading must return a pending outcome until verified local content is ready; a failed load should become a correlated refusal with the DHT reason as data.\n    49\t\n    50\tWire the ticker to re-check **on a relevant load completion or failure event**, and preserve the pending request’s identity. Avoid re-checking every pending run on every generic node event: unrelated traffic would consume function-policy checks and may cause needless repeated links. A bounded re-check after a relevant event should also handle multiple pending module loads.\n    51\t\n    52\tDeferring `:lease` is sound. A DHT request budget supplies a bounded fetch outcome, but it is **not** a lease for the whole pending run. State that distinction. Test failures on all four VMs, including the register fallback, as well as successful equality.\n    53\t\n    54\t## 6. Slices L0–L5 — **AGREE-WITH-CHANGES**\n    55\t\n    56\tThe broad order works, with these gates changed:\n    57\t\n    58\t| Slice | Acceptance that must be added or corrected |\n    59\t|---|---|\n    60\t| **L0** | Keep the publisher plain Clojure and host neutral. Verify canonical signature vectors across every supported host; tampering with envelope, proof, name, and manifest must fail. |\n    61\t| **L1** | Generic staged loading must distinguish a signaled missing blob from an invalid walk, preserve DHT miss reasons, and prove a fetched blob is verified before reporting loaded. |\n    62\t| **L2** | Exercise a missing manifest, row, derivation record, each format image, and a transitive requirement. Linking one format successfully does **not** prove the complete four-format closure was loaded. |\n    63\t| **L3** | Test a pending require completing after a DHT event with **no typed line**; test unrelated events, repeated events, abandonment, late answers, and function link policies. “Solo node refuses at once” is too strong if the node first has to exhaust a bounded lookup. |\n    64\t| **L4** | Derive names from a specified set of loaded immutable snapshots. Test undeclared, bad signature, conflicting addresses, same-address multi-asserter behavior, retraction, and stale snapshots. Materialize rows on explicit publish under the decision above. |\n    65\t| **L5** | Process A publishes; B receives an index-manifest address, loads it, resolves and evaluates by name. Run plain Clojure through the **same load API** used by the yin.repl host functions, including failure results. Cover all four VMs across the supported hosts, rather than only semantic and stack on JVM/Node. |\n    66\t\n    67\tThe host-function reuse invariant needs an explicit interface gate: the REPL host module should only adapt effect requests and responses to the plain Clojure `join → load-index → derive-names → load-module → link` operations. It must not contain a second loader. The node remains peer to peer; publication permission and chosen trust roots are per-node composition, not privileged network roles. `dao.jing` remains passive byte storage, and code remains represented by datoms even when link images are cached as content.\n    68\t\n    69\t## 7. Consolidated OWNER decisions\n    70\t\n    71\t| # | Second-opinion recommendation |\n    72\t|---|---|\n    73\t| **1. Rows per round** | **Disagree with lead.** Materialize module rows on explicit publish. Resolve the existing `:yin/address` retrieval contract separately. |\n    74\t| **2. Signatures and Dart** | **Agree with signatures for DHT names.** Specify canonical bytes and cross-host vectors first. If Dart cannot verify in this slice, permit direct-address linking there and do not claim Dart name resolution is complete. |\n    75\t| **3. Granularity** | **Agree:** explicit named-export publication derived from indexed code. Define how multiple defining programs, free names, and transitive requirements form one module before implementation. |\n    76\t| **4. Network on require** | **Agree conditionally:** an already configured DHT link source may start a bounded load on `require`; expose pending and failure as data, and state this behavior in the REPL help/banner. |\n    77\t| **5. Name scope** | **Agree with folding explicitly loaded snapshots**, provided the composition records their addresses and has deterministic handling of repeated assertions and conflicts. Do not silently include every blob in the local store. |\n    78\t| **6. Key material** | **Choose a loaded, stable key for durable publisher identity.** An ephemeral per-process key is useful for tests or temporary sessions, but printing one does not make a persistent name trustworthy across restarts. Define key loss and replacement behavior. |\n    79\t| **7. Deferrals** | **Agree:** persistent stepped ring linker, latest-root discovery, and lease policy can wait. Record the staged loader as an interim implementation with a closure-walk acceptance gate. |\n    80\t\n    81\t**Missing owner rulings:** decide whether same-address assertions from multiple declared principals count as consensus or ambiguity; whether an index’s `:yin/address` guarantees row retrieval before explicit module publication; and whether the first release promises signed name loading on Dart or only direct-address loading there.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
