{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '550,685p' docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | nl -ba -v550\"","aggregated_output":"   550\tJVM facade, the `:jing/unacknowledged` completion in `dao.jing.content.step`.\n   551\tPeers are wired through ring buffers carrying raw datagram values; S2 needs\n   552\tno socket and no S1. It speaks the section 7 wire for single-datagram\n   553\tmessages and implements the whole cookie protocol of section 8 (issue, echo,\n   554\tverify, the gate, the size rule, padding, freshness) with the S2 stand-in\n   555\t`cookie-for`. Chunks are S3; the keyed MAC is S4. Acceptance:\n   556\t- An acknowledged write used, for every counted peer, the cookie that peer's\n   557\t  fresh reply carried; a store with a missing or wrong cookie causes no work.\n   558\t- The need-cookie reply never encodes to more than `first-contact-bytes`, and\n   559\t  a cookie-less datagram shorter than that reply is answered with silence.\n   560\t- Requests and answers are the exact `:jing/*` shapes; the unmodified content\n   561\t  clients drive a DHT.\n   562\t- With a warm table a write is acknowledged in the step that read it, after\n   563\t  exactly `ack-peers` counted sends, and the fact says `/sent`.\n   564\t- `put-bytes-fn` performs no lookup and no peer wait; the publish/readback\n   565\t  round completes against `:local`.\n   566\t- Each `/unacknowledged` reason of 4.3 is produced by a test, including cold\n   567\t  bootstrap reaching `too-few-peers` at the tick deadline and then\n   568\t  succeeding once peers are proven.\n   569\t- Solo mode composes no socket streams and holds no pending work.\n   570\t- A file-backed local verdict survives restart.\n   571\t- Time advances only by appended ticks; no test sleeps.\n   572\t- The JVM facade has one step owner and respects deadlines.\n   573\t- `dao.jing.md`'s backend list and status are updated.\n   574\t\n   575\t**S3 — real sockets and chunks.** Depends on S1 and S2. Chunk records and\n   576\tthe move onto `dao.stream.datagram` sockets. Loopback only. Acceptance:\n   577\t- Two and three peers exchange small queries and multi-datagram segments\n   578\t  under injected loss and reordering.\n   579\t- The largest legal `:find` reply fits one datagram.\n   580\t- Oversize refuses before any send; malformed, duplicate and inconsistent\n   581\t  chunks stay within every bound.\n   582\t- A chunked write counts a peer only when every chunk answered `ok`.\n   583\t- **One transport-neutral chunk split/absorb utility is used by both\n   584\t  `dao.stream.udp` and the DHT**, each keeping its own wire fields and keys.\n   585\t  **Declared fallback:** if the two identities resist clean parameterization,\n   586\t  the DHT ships its own reassembly and `dao.stream.udp` is left untouched.\n   587\t  The slice report says which was taken and why.\n   588\t- Real covered-index nodes are measured against `max-message-bytes`.\n   589\t- JVM↔Node and JVM↔Dart pass the same wire tests.\n   590\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (Remote and DHT) is updated to this wire.\n   591\t- `dao.jing`'s Base64 functions are repointed onto `dao.stream.base64`.\n   592\t\n   593\t**S4 — hardening, lookup repair, storage bound.** Depends on S3. The keyed\n   594\t`cookie-for` replacing the S2 stand-in, the chunk path under the gate, and\n   595\tevery bound. Acceptance:\n   596\t- A cookie cannot be computed without the secret; the S2 stand-in is gone.\n   597\t- A spoofed source elicits at most one reply, never larger than the datagram\n   598\t  it sent, and silence when the reply would be larger; it allocates nothing.\n   599\t  Tested across datagram sizes from 1 byte to the budget, chunks included.\n   600\t- A reply from an unexpected source is ignored; a claimed address never\n   601\t  enters the table.\n   602\t- A live next-nearest peer is tried after dead peers, and dead peers leave\n   603\t  the table.\n   604\t- Inbound storage at its bound refuses explicitly.\n   605\t- `publish?` false neither originates nor serves.\n   606\t- Non-loopback exposure is permitted only after this slice.\n   607\t\n   608\t**S5 — REPL integration.** Depends on S4 and on the durable-store epic (spec,\n   609\tHEAD, lock, recovery). Acceptance:\n   610\t- Default `mem`; `dht:<dir>` is explicit; with no peers it is solo and opens\n   611\t  no socket.\n   612\t- Publishing requires its own flag, separate from peers, and the REPL states\n   613\t  what will be shared before it shares.\n   614\t- The REPL shows per publication whether it is acknowledged (sent to N\n   615\t  peers) or not and why; no round waits on the network.\n   616\t- Two processes with separate locked directories exchange content.\n   617\t- A reader given a manifest address hydrates and queries a remote index.\n   618\t\n   619\t### The plain Clojure path (S5, owner direction 2026-10-01)\n   620\t\n   621\tOwner, verbatim: \"plain clojure code should be able to query for code in the\n   622\tdht. the yin.repl should use the same path as the clojure repl via\n   623\thost-functions\".\n   624\t\n   625\t`dao.space.dht` (CLJC; JVM, Node, Dart) is that path. It lives under\n   626\t`dao.space` because it joins a `dao.jing.dht` node to `dao.space.index` and\n   627\t`dao.space.query`; putting it under `dao.jing` would make the\n   628\tpayload-agnostic well aware of index structure. It has no `yin.repl`\n   629\tdependency.\n   630\t\n   631\t- `(join opts)` composes a node over `:local` (a `dao.jing` store the\n   632\t  caller has opened, and locked if it is a directory) or `:dir`, which\n   633\t  `join` opens as the durable directory store `dao.space.store/open`:\n   634\t  exclusively, under its directory lock (OS locks on the JVM and Dart,\n   635\t  claim entries on Node, and the in-process registry), so a second owner in\n   636\t  this or another process is refused naming the directory; `close!`\n   637\t  releases it. The REPL's `file:<dir>` and `dht:<dir>` stores open the\n   638\t  same store, so a REPL and a plain node never share a directory. Options:\n   639\t  `:peers`\n   640\t  (none is solo: no socket, no secret), `:publish?` (default false, never\n   641\t  implied by peers), `:bind-host` (default `127.0.0.1`), `:bind-port`\n   642\t  (default 0), `:max-inbound-bytes` (default 64 MiB), `:bind!` (the host\n   643\t  datagram seam; each build's own by default). A node with peers mints its\n   644\t  root secret at join: 32 CSPRNG bytes, held only in the node value.\n   645\t- `(step node now)` is the node's only advance; `now` is its owner's\n   646\t  nondecreasing millisecond reading, appended as a tick. It answers\n   647\t  `[node events]`: `:bound`, `:bind-failed`, `:published` (per announced\n   648\t  publication: acknowledged with the peers sent to, or not with the reason\n   649\t  and peers reached, from the `/sent` and `/unacknowledged` facts),\n   650\t  `:publication-unknown`, `:loaded`, `:load-failed`.\n   651\t- `(store node)` is the node's byte-store handle (5.1); `(announce! node\n   652\t  manifest)` closes the publication its puts made.\n   653\t- `(load-index node manifest)` starts a load; `step` walks the index through\n   654\t  the local store with `dao.space.index/read-manifest` and fetches each\n   655\t  missing blob with a `:jing/get` through `dao.jing.content.step`, until all\n   656\t  four covered indexes read back and cover the manifest's count.\n   657\t- `(db node manifest)` is `dao.space.query/published-db` over the loaded\n   658\t  index (`restored-indexes`); `(q node manifest query & inputs)` is\n   659\t  `dao.space.query/q` over its current view.\n   660\t\n   661\t`yin.repl` composes its `dht:<dir>` store with `join` (over its locked\n   662\tdurable store), and its `dao.space.dht` host module answers `load-index`,\n   663\t`load-status` and `q` with these functions (`yin.repl.dao.space-index.md`,\n   664\t\"DHT store\").\n   665\t\n   666\tOut of this epic: pinning, garbage collection, availability repair and\n   667\treconciliation sweeps, NAT meeting, a browser transport, latest-root\n   668\tdiscovery, authenticated node ids.\n   669\t\n   670\t**Loading code for evaluation** is the next epic, specified in\n   671\t[`yin.vm.linker.dht.md`](./yin.vm.linker.dht.md). It generalizes\n   672\t`load-index` into `dao.space.dht/load` (a staged load over any walk, with\n   673\tfailure reasons as data), adds a replicate backlog so a round's many row\n   674\twrites never report `/busy`, and makes the node's store the linker's\n   675\tcontent source. It changes nothing in sections 2 to 9.\n   676\t\n   677\t## Relationship to the intake-pool observer\n   678\t\n   679\tThe DHT is a content-store backend, not a stream transport. The intake-pool\n   680\tobserver (`dao.jing/observer-state` / `observe-step!`) materializes whatever\n   681\tthe intake streams carry; whether the content store behind it is a DHT handle\n   682\tis invisible to the observer. Conversely, the DHT replicates content, never\n   683\tstreams: there is no ordered, push-based replication of a writer's log through\n   684\tthis layer and no single-writer authority to coordinate — each node simply\n   685\tstores and fetches content-addressed payloads.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"agent_message","text":"## Verdict: SIGN-OFF WITHHELD\n\nThe draft adopts the owner’s four explicit decisions and most of the AGREE-WITH-CHANGES corrections. The closure walker, signed name format, cross-host vectors, event-driven recheck, and L0–L5 tests are substantially more concrete than the lead design. The issues below still leave incompatible implementations possible.\n\n| Severity | File:line | Issue | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High | [yin.vm.linker.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-dht/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:262) | The replicate backlog has no defined capacity, admission result, ordering, or cancellation rule. Retrying `/busy` “on a later step” can grow an unbounded queue while the DHT permits only 64 pending writes. L4’s one large program test would not catch sustained input or failure. | Specify a bounded backlog and what happens when it fills; define retry fairness and publication accounting. Test multiple consecutive rounds, including a peer that never accepts writes. |\n| High | [yin.vm.linker.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-dht/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:266) | A publication is “reported” when blobs are `/sent` **or refused**, but the status and event shape do not say whether the result is acknowledged, partially published, or unacknowledged. An index can advertise `:yin/address` rows that peers cannot fetch, contrary to the owner’s retrieval contract. | Define the publication result for each failed blob and its effect on the announced index and `:yin/address` guarantee. Add an acceptance case with a failed row replication. |\n| High | [yin.vm.linker.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-dht/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:575) | Requiring dependency assertions “in the same index” adds a trust and publication condition absent from the pinned-address closure. A dependency may belong to another publisher’s signed snapshot; the draft neither says how its assertion is copied into this index nor how the reader declares and loads it. L4’s dependency test does not exercise a second principal or snapshot. | Specify the exact assertion and snapshot procedure for a foreign dependency, or remove the same-index rule and require resolution from the explicit snapshot set. Test both matching and missing or conflicting dependency bindings. |\n| Medium | [yin.vm.linker.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-dht/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:217) | The generic load failure has `:unaskable`, but section 9 maps only miss and invalid walks to link refusals. Hosts can choose different require errors for an append failure. The `:invalid` row also allows arbitrary exception messages as defects, so its portable shape is underspecified. | Give `:unaskable` a link response shape and test it on all hosts. Define a closed defect code with optional diagnostic text for throwing walks. |\n| Medium | [yin.vm.linker.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-dht/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:337) | The module footprint is mandatory, yet the draft delegates its computation to the dependency-completion document and tells engineers to stop if unavailable. That is an honest open item, but it blocks L0’s publisher contract and L4’s derived publication acceptance. | State the precise input and result of footprint computation here, or make completion of that dependency an explicit prerequisite and acceptance gate before L0/L4. |\n\n**Judgment of the additions:** Retraction followed by assertion matches the existing fold; same-address consensus and key-file behavior honor the owner. Limiting module-level cross-definition reads to stack and register faithfully describes the current linker. Treating an encoder change as signature-breaking is accurate for the specified signed bytes, though it is a compatibility commitment. A synchronous Dart Ed25519 implementation is a valid requirement; the chosen package and its JVM/Node/Dart vector results must be established in L0. The footprint deferral is the remaining blocker to a frozen publisher contract."}}
